Submit the V4L2 stateless controls the driver actually advertises.

Chromium always told the H.264 decoder to run frame-based and always
prefixed slices with a 3-byte Annex-B start code. Apple's AVD, and the
other mainline stateless decoders, are slice-based and want the raw NAL
plus V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_SLICE_PARAMS. The out-of-range decode mode
fails the first frame in userspace, so Chromium falls back to FFmpeg.

HEVC on AVD is frame-based, but the hardware does not parse slice
headers. Chromium never sent V4L2_CID_STATELESS_HEVC_SLICE_PARAMS, so
the driver queued an empty job and the frame timed out. The same slice
parameters are what hantro, rkvdec and cedrus need. Start codes are
prepended only when the driver says it wants Annex-B.

The queried default is left in place for a frame-based device, so
ChromeOS hardware keeps the old path. A picture with more than one
H.264 slice on a slice-based device is rejected for now: that needs one
media request per slice and V4L2_BUF_FLAG_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF. The
x264 test clips are one slice per picture. HEVC stays one request per
picture, which is what AVD's frame-based mode asks for.

VP9 profile 2 is unchanged. The init path already sets 10-bit and both
subsampling flags; that stall needs a trace on the device.

